Fill each plus-shaped background notch with the color of the frame side it opens toward.

Input structure
- A one-cell-thick rectangular frame has four solid-colored sides and neutral corner pixels.
- The frame interior contains a dominant background color and one foreground object color.
- Some object boundaries contain five-pixel plus-shaped background notches.

Rule
- Find every background plus whose opposite side is supported by foreground object pixels and whose open side faces one frame side.
- Recolor the five pixels of that plus with the color of the facing frame side.
- Leave all other pixels unchanged.

Key insight
The frame side colors are direction labels for the open notches in the foreground object.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="62593bfd" lines="32-46">
<description>
Sort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.

Rule:
- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).
- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).
- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).
- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.

Key insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="6165ea8f" lines="74-90">
<description>
Build a color-labeled shape-equivalence table from the objects listed on the right edge.

Input structure:
- The background contains one main 4-connected object for each foreground color.
- The rightmost column has one singleton pixel of each foreground color, listed top-to-bottom.
- Those singleton pixels define the row and column order of the output table.

Rule:
- Normalize each main object to its tight mask and compare masks up to rotation or reflection.
- Draw 2x2 color labels across the top and down the left, separated by one background row/column.
- For each off-diagonal table cell, paint a 2x2 red block when the two labeled objects have the same shape under rotation/reflection, otherwise paint a 2x2 grey block.
- Leave diagonal cells and all separators as background.

Key insight:
- Object positions and original orientations are distractors; the relation is dihedral shape equivalence, while the right-edge legend fixes the table order.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="e39e9282" lines="73-81">
<description>
Project maroon markers into adjacent grey or pink 3x3 squares, erasing grey squares afterward.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background, solid 3x3 grey and/or pink squares, and maroon marker pixels or segments directly touching one side of exactly one square.
Rule
Grey squares are erased; their adjacent maroon markers stay in place and stamp the nearest edge cell inside the grey square. Pink squares stay pink; their adjacent maroon markers disappear and stamp the corresponding cell on the square's middle row or middle column.
Key insight
The side touched by a marker determines the projection: grey uses the near edge, while pink uses the centerline, preserving the marker's coordinate along that side.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="58c02a16" lines="10-26">
<description>
Expand the top-left seed mask into a recursive colored pattern and tile it across the full grid.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- All foreground lies in a top-left rectangle whose last row and last column are a solid secondary color.
- Removing that secondary-color row and column leaves a seed region containing background plus one primary color.

Rule
- Use the primary-color pixels in the seed as a binary mask.
- Replace each primary seed cell with a copy of the seed mask in the primary color, and each background seed cell
  with a copy of the seed mask in the secondary color.
- Repeat and crop that expanded tile to the original grid shape.

Key insight
The solid L-shaped secondary-color border is a legend, not output content; it supplies the color used when the
seed's background cells are expanded.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="9f41bd9c" lines="10-30">
<description>
Move the side-attached foreground object to the opposite wall, shear its lower rows inward, and paint the crossed floor cells.

Input structure:
- The top row is the upper background color and the bottom row is the
  floor color.
- A single non-background, non-floor object touches exactly one vertical
  grid edge and rests immediately above the first solid floor row.

Rule:
- Clear the source object to the upper background color.
- Repaint each object row on the opposite side: the first three relative
  rows keep the edge alignment, and each lower row shifts one more pixel
  inward from that side.
- On the first floor row, repaint the traversed side-to-object segment
  maroon, stopping just before the final outer bottom support pixel.

Key insight:
- The side of the source object gives the horizontal direction, while the
  row index inside the object gives the one-cell-per-row diagonal shear.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="c920a713" lines="87-102">
<description>
Untangle overlapping colored rectangular frames into concentric square frames ordered by their visible crossings.

Input structure
- The background contains several one-cell-thick axis-aligned rectangular frames, one color per frame.
- Frames overlap, and the visible color at an overlap records a directed crossing from the hidden frame to the visible frame.
- Each foreground color still has visible pixels on the perimeter of its full rectangular bounding box.

Rule
- Infer each color's full rectangular frame from the bounding box of its visible pixels.
- Add a directed edge A -> B when B is visible at a crossing of A and B's frame perimeters.
- Find the unique path that visits every frame color once, then render those colors as one-pixel-thick concentric square frames from outside to inside.

Key insight
- The output is not a crop of the input; the crossing graph gives a single frame order, and that order is redrawn as nested rings.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="46c35fc7" lines="21-34">
<description>
Rotate the colors around every 3x3 foreground ring, with corners and side-centers cycling in opposite directions.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background color.
- Each 4-connected foreground object is a complete 3x3 ring: eight foreground pixels around a background center.

Rule
- For each ring, move corner colors one step around the four corners.
- Move side-center colors one step around the four side centers in the opposite direction.
- Leave the background center and all other background pixels unchanged.

Key insight
- Corners and side centers form two separate four-cycles on the same 3x3 ring.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="6bcdb01e" lines="10-24">
<description>
Extend the edge-touching green start bar through the corridor, turning at each teal blocker until the path leaves the grid.

Input structure:
- The background is the most common color.
- Exactly two foreground colors are present: a 2-pixel 4-connected start bar and many blocker pixels.
- Exactly one pixel of the start bar lies on the grid border.

Rule:
- Start from the border pixel of the bar and move through the other bar pixel into the grid.
- Paint every traversed non-blocker pixel with the start color.
- When the next cell is a blocker, turn to the unique perpendicular non-blocker neighbor; when the next cell is outside the grid, stop.

Key insight: The blocker pixels define a one-cell-wide corridor, so every interior collision has exactly one valid 90-degree turn.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

